/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccnp-encor.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addPlanDetail("ccnp-encor", {"study":{"1":[["When does a collapsed core make sense and when do you need a separate core layer?","A collapsed core (two-tier) fits small or single-building campuses. A separate core helps when many distribution blocks must be interconnected, because it keeps the design scalable and avoids a full mesh between distribution pairs."],["Name the four SD-WAN components and what each does.","Manager (formerly vManage): management and monitoring. Validator (formerly vBond): authenticates and orchestrates joining devices. Controllers (formerly vSmart): control plane, OMP routes and policy. WAN Edges: data plane, IPsec tunnels carrying user traffic."],["Map SD-Access planes to protocols.","Control plane: LISP. Data plane: VXLAN. Policy plane: Cisco TrustSec (SGTs). Management: Catalyst Center."],["Shaping vs policing?","Shaping buffers excess traffic and sends it later, adding delay but avoiding drops; it is used outbound. Policing drops or remarks excess traffic right away and can be used in either direction."],["What do the RIB, FIB and adjacency table each hold?","RIB: all best routes from routing protocols. FIB: CEF's copy of the RIB optimized for forwarding lookups. Adjacency table: the Layer 2 rewrite information for each next hop."]],"2":[["Type 1 vs type 2 hypervisor, and where do containers fit?","Type 1 runs on bare metal (ESXi, KVM, Hyper-V); type 2 runs on a host OS (VirtualBox, Workstation). Containers share the host kernel and package only the app and its dependencies."],["What is VRF-Lite and what does it not provide?","Multiple routing tables on one router without MPLS. It does not carry VRF labels across the network, so each hop needs its own VRF interfaces or subinterfaces."],["What causes GRE recursive routing and how do you fix it?","The router learns the route to the tunnel destination through the tunnel. Make sure the underlay path to the destination is preferred and not advertised through the tunnel's routing protocol."],["Why GRE over IPsec instead of plain IPsec?","Plain crypto-map IPsec does not carry multicast, so routing protocols cannot run over it. GRE carries multicast and IPsec encrypts the GRE packets."],["LISP EID vs RLOC; VXLAN VNI size.","EID identifies the endpoint, RLOC is the tunnel router's location. The VXLAN VNI is 24 bits, about 16 million segments, carried in UDP port 4789."]],"3":[["Which DTP mode combinations form a trunk?","Trunk with trunk, desirable or auto; desirable with desirable or auto. Auto with auto stays access. Nonegotiate disables DTP and needs the other side hard-set to trunk."],["What must match for an MST region and what happens if it doesn't?","Region name, revision and VLAN-to-instance mapping. If anything differs, switches treat each other as separate regions and interact only through the common spanning tree (CIST)."],["Which OSPF LSAs are blocked in stub, totally stubby and NSSA areas?","Stub: Type 4 and 5 blocked, default via Type 3. Totally stubby: Type 3, 4 and 5 blocked except a default. NSSA: Type 5 blocked but internal ASBRs can originate Type 7, which the ABR converts to Type 5."],["List the first steps of Cisco BGP best-path selection.","Highest weight, highest local preference, locally originated, shortest AS path, lowest origin code (IGP < EGP < incomplete), lowest MED, eBGP over iBGP, then lowest IGP metric to the next hop."],["What is the EIGRP feasibility condition?","A neighbor's reported distance must be lower than the current feasible distance. Only paths that meet it are feasible successors, which guarantees they are loop-free."],["How does PIM-SM build its trees?","Receivers join a shared tree rooted at the RP; sources register with the RP. The last-hop router can then switch to the shortest path tree toward the source. SSM skips the RP using IGMPv3 (S,G) joins."]],"4":[["Syslog severity levels 0–7?","0 emergencies, 1 alerts, 2 critical, 3 errors, 4 warnings, 5 notifications, 6 informational, 7 debugging. Setting a level includes it and all lower numbers."],["SPAN vs RSPAN vs ERSPAN?","SPAN copies to a port on the same switch. RSPAN carries copies in a dedicated VLAN across Layer 2 trunks. ERSPAN encapsulates copies in GRE across routed networks."],["What are the three parts of a Flexible NetFlow configuration?","A flow record (key and non-key fields), a flow exporter (collector address and port) and a flow monitor that ties them together and is applied to an interface."],["NETCONF vs RESTCONF?","NETCONF: SSH on port 830, XML, operations like get-config and edit-config, candidate datastores. RESTCONF: HTTPS, JSON or XML, HTTP methods mapped to YANG data."],["How do IP SLA and object tracking remove a static route?","IP SLA probes a target; a track object follows the probe's reachability; the static route references the track and is withdrawn when the track goes down."]],"5":[["TACACS+ vs RADIUS?","TACACS+: TCP 49, encrypts the whole body, separates authentication and authorization, good for device administration. RADIUS: UDP 1812/1813, encrypts only the password, combines authentication and authorization, good for network access."],["When does an AAA method list fall back to the next method?","Only when the current method returns an error, such as no response from the server. A rejection (wrong password) ends the attempt."],["What does CoPP protect and how?","The route processor. It applies an MQC policy to the control-plane interface so traffic destined to the device is classified and rate-limited."],["802.1X vs MAB vs WebAuth?","802.1X uses a supplicant and EAP credentials. MAB uses the MAC address for devices without a supplicant. WebAuth uses a browser portal, typically for guests."],["TrustSec vs MACsec?","TrustSec classifies traffic into groups (SGTs) and enforces group-based SGACLs. MACsec (802.1AE) encrypts Ethernet frames hop by hop on a link."]],"6":[["Map HTTP methods to CRUD.","POST create, GET read, PUT replace, PATCH update, DELETE delete."],["What do 200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403 and 404 mean?","200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized (bad or missing credentials), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not allowed), 404 Not Found."],["How does a script authenticate to the Catalyst Center API?","POST to the token endpoint with basic authentication, then send the returned token in the X-Auth-Token header on later requests."],["Agent-based vs agentless tools?","Puppet and Chef use agents on managed nodes that pull from a server. Ansible is agentless and pushes over SSH or APIs, which suits network devices."],["What guardrails should apply when AI tools act on the network?","Least-privilege access, human approval for changes, logging of every action, change windows and a tested rollback. Treat AI output as a proposal until verified."]]},"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Architecture","w":15,"topics":["Enterprise design: two-tier (collapsed core) and three-tier campus, fabric/spine-leaf, cloud vs on-premises","High availability: redundancy, first hop redundancy protocols, stateful switchover (SSO)","SD-WAN control and data plane: Manager, Validator, Controllers, WAN Edges, OMP and IPsec tunnels","SD-WAN benefits and limitations compared with traditional WAN","SD-Access: control plane (LISP), data plane (VXLAN), policy plane (TrustSec), fabric node roles","Traditional campus vs SD-Access: underlay and overlay, group-based policy from Catalyst Center","QoS components: classification and marking (DSCP, CoS), policing, shaping, queuing (CBWFQ, LLQ), WRED","Hardware and software switching: process switching, CEF, FIB, RIB, adjacency table","CAM and TCAM tables and what each stores","Punted traffic and its effect on the control plane CPU"],"notes":["Exam topics section 1.0 (v1.2 removed the wireless architecture topics)"],"labs":["Draw a two-tier and a three-tier campus for the same 3-building site in draw.io, then write down which layer holds routing, FHRP and access policy in each.","In GNS3 or Cisco Modeling Labs Free, apply an MQC policy with a priority class for DSCP EF and a shaper on a WAN interface, then read show policy-map interface while generating traffic.","On an IOS router, compare show ip route, show ip cef and show adjacency detail for the same prefix, and note which table supplies the next hop and which the Layer 2 rewrite."]},{"id":2,"name":"Virtualization","w":10,"topics":["Hypervisors: type 1 vs type 2, virtual machines and virtual switching","Containers compared with virtual machines","VRF and VRF-Lite: separate routing tables, overlapping addresses, per-VRF routing","GRE tunnels: configuration, keepalives, recursive routing problems, MTU and MSS","IPsec: IKE phases, tunnel vs transport mode, crypto maps vs tunnel protection","GRE over IPsec and virtual tunnel interfaces for routing protocols over VPNs","LISP: EID, RLOC, map server and map resolver, ITR and ETR","VXLAN: VNI, VTEP, UDP encapsulation, overlay vs underlay"],"notes":["Exam topics section 2.0"],"labs":["In GNS3 or CML Free, put two customer LANs with the same 10.1.1.0/24 subnet into VRFs CUST-A and CUST-B on one router and verify with show ip route vrf.","Build a GRE tunnel between two routers, run OSPF over it, then trigger recursive routing on purpose and fix it with a better underlay route.","Protect the GRE tunnel with IPsec using tunnel protection, then confirm encryption counters with show crypto ipsec sa while pinging across the tunnel."]},{"id":3,"name":"Infrastructure","w":30,"topics":["Layer 2: static and dynamic 802.1Q trunking (DTP), allowed VLANs and native VLAN","EtherChannel: LACP, PAgP and static; member consistency; load balancing","Spanning tree: RSTP and MST, port roles, root placement, BPDU guard, root guard, loop guard","EIGRP vs OSPF: algorithms, metrics, path selection, load balancing, summarization","EIGRP: feasible successors, variance, stub routing","OSPFv2 and OSPFv3: multi-area, adjacencies, network types, area types (stub, totally stubby, NSSA), summarization and filtering","eBGP between directly connected neighbors: neighbor states and best-path selection","Policy-based routing with route maps","IP services: NTP and PTP, NAT and PAT, HSRP and VRRP","Multicast: IGMPv2/v3, PIM sparse mode, RP, RPF check, SSM"],"notes":["Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2, Layer 3, IP services; v1.2 expanded multicast)"],"labs":["In CML Free or GNS3, build a three-switch triangle with MST (two instances), set a different root per instance and check show spanning-tree mst for port roles.","Build a three-area OSPF network with a totally stubby area and an NSSA, then compare show ip ospf database on routers in each area.","Connect two routers in different ASes with eBGP, advertise loopbacks, then change local preference and weight and watch show ip bgp change the best path."]},{"id":4,"name":"Network Assurance","w":10,"topics":["Diagnose problems with debugs, conditional debugs, ping and traceroute","SNMP versions (v2c vs v3 authPriv), traps and polling","Syslog severity levels and logging configuration","Flexible NetFlow: flow records, exporters and monitors","SPAN, RSPAN and ERSPAN","IP SLA probes and object tracking","Catalyst Center (formerly DNA Center) workflows: assurance, health scores, AI-driven insights","NETCONF and RESTCONF for configuration and operational data"],"notes":["Exam topics section 4.0"],"labs":["Configure Flexible NetFlow on a router in GNS3 or CML Free and export to a free collector such as ntopng Community or a nfdump container.","Set up IP SLA ICMP echo with track and a static default route, then break reachability and watch the route disappear with show ip route and show track.","Enable RESTCONF on a Catalyst 8000v in the Cisco DevNet always-on sandbox and GET the interface list with curl or Postman."]},{"id":5,"name":"Security","w":20,"topics":["Device access control: line and local user authentication, SSH-only VTY access","AAA with TACACS+ and RADIUS, method lists and fallback","Infrastructure security: standard and extended ACLs, placement and order","Control Plane Policing (CoPP)","REST API security: HTTPS, tokens, secret handling","Network security design: threat defense, endpoint security, next-generation firewall","Cisco TrustSec (SGT, SGACL) and MACsec","Network access control: 802.1X, MAB and WebAuth","Layer 2 protections: DHCP snooping, dynamic ARP inspection, IP source guard"],"notes":["Exam topics section 5.0 (v1.2 removed the wireless security topics)"],"labs":["Lock down a router in GNS3 or CML Free: local users, SSH only, AAA method list with a (deliberately unreachable) TACACS+ server and local fallback; test the fallback.","Write a CoPP policy that permits OSPF and SSH at high rates and polices ICMP to the router, then ping-flood it and read show policy-map control-plane.","Configure 802.1X and MAB on a switch port with FreeRADIUS on a Linux VM and watch show access-session interface detail as clients connect."]},{"id":6,"name":"Automation and AI","w":15,"topics":["Python basics: variables, loops, functions, dictionaries, the requests library","JSON syntax and parsing JSON in Python","YANG data models and how NETCONF and RESTCONF use them","REST API methods and response codes (200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 500)","Catalyst Center Intent APIs: token authentication and common calls","SD-WAN Manager REST APIs","Embedded Event Manager (EEM) applets","Orchestration tools: agent-based (Puppet, Chef) vs agentless (Ansible)","AI in network operations: baselining, anomaly detection, AI-assisted troubleshooting and safe guardrails"],"notes":["Exam topics section 6.0 (renamed Automation and Artificial Intelligence in v1.2)"],"labs":["Write a Python script with requests that gets a token from the Cisco DevNet Catalyst Center sandbox and prints each device's hostname and software version.","Write an EEM applet in GNS3 or CML Free that emails or logs a message and copies the running config to flash whenever the configuration changes.","Write an Ansible playbook with the cisco.ios collection that sets NTP servers and a login banner on two lab routers, then run it twice to see idempotence."]}]});
